<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/intermediate/eastern-philosophy">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    "Eastern philosophy" names several rigorous traditions — <strong>Indian (Vedanta, Buddhism,
    Nyaya logic), Chinese (Confucianism, Daoism), Japanese (Zen)</strong> — that developed
    logic, ethics, metaphysics and philosophy of mind in parallel with the West, often earlier,
    sometimes deeper. Reading them seriously (not as exotic decoration) corrects the Western
    canon's blind spots on self, mind and method.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four traditions and their central moves" widget="equationStep" config={eastCfg} />

  <h2>Where East and West genuinely meet</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Anatta meets bundle theory:</strong> Buddhism's "no permanent self" and Hume's bundle of perceptions converge — and neuroscience's distributed self-models (the neuroscience ladder) give both an empirical foothold.</li>
    <li><strong>Emptiness and anti-essentialism:</strong> Nagarjuna's arguments that things lack independent essence parallel modern anti-essentialism and structuralism — logic, not mysticism.</li>
    <li><strong>Ethics of relationship vs principles:</strong> Confucian role-ethics prefigures virtue ethics and care ethics — and challenges Kantian universality with something the West largely lost (embedded, contextual moral life).</li>
    <li><strong>Contemplative science:</strong> meditation research (attention, neuroplasticity) turned first-person traditions into third-person study — collaboration, where done carefully, that neither side should rush.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> the Upanishads; Nagarjuna, Mulamadhyamakakarika; Confucius, Analects; Van Norden, Taking Back Philosophy (2017).</p>
</TopicPage>
